---
title: Crêpes
description: Thin Flemish pancakes with vanilla sugar and melted butter through the batter, served flat with butter and brown sugar or rolled around a filling.
author: Jeroen Meus
source: https://dagelijksekost.vrt.be/gerechten/pannenkoeken-met-een-geurige-kop-chocolademelk
course: breakfast
cuisine: belgian
diet: vegetarian
difficulty: easy
servings: 4
yield: 10%crêpes
time: 45m
prep time: 10m
cook time: 20m
tags: [pancakes, pan-fried]
---

Melt @unsalted butter{40%g} in a #small saucepan{} over low heat and set it aside to cool slightly.

Put @whole milk{500%ml}, @plain flour{200%g}, @egg{3}, @sugar{1%tbsp}, @vanilla sugar{10%g}, @salt{1%pinch} and the melted butter into a #blender{} and blend until completely smooth.

By hand instead: whisk the eggs into the flour first to make a thick paste, then let the milk down into it gradually. Going the other way round, thin to thick, is how you get lumps.

Rest the batter for ~resting{30%minutes}. The flour hydrates and the gluten relaxes, which is the difference between a supple pancake and a rubbery one.

Heat a #26cm non-stick pan{} over medium-high heat and wipe it out with @neutral oil{1%tsp} on kitchen paper.

Pour in @batter{80%ml} and swirl the pan straight away so it runs out to the rim in a thin, even layer.

Cook ~{60%seconds}, until the edges lift and go lacy and the underside is patched with brown. Flip and give the second side ~{30%seconds}.

Serve flat or folded into quarters with @butter{to taste} and @brown sugar{to taste}, or @Liège syrup{to taste}.

> For thicker pancakes, add 50 g more flour (250 g in total).

> The batter keeps overnight in the fridge and is arguably better for it. Whisk it before use, since the flour settles.
